      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A novel incubation unit is described that is highly suitable for thorough studies of oxygen deprivation states. Its application with cultured heart cells is experimentally demonstrated. The release of enzymes, taken as a marker for cell damage, has clearly shown that restriction of the volume of extracellular medium combined with oxygen plus glucose deprivation caused greatest cellular damage. It may be considered as an experimental ischemia-like state. Furthermore, the onset of cellular damage followed a time table very much like that occurring in vivo under similar conditions, more so than any other previously described studies. A time lag between the release of cytoplasmic enzymes and lysosomal enzymes and other observations made in the present study suggests a sequential order of events in which the release of cytoplasmic enzymes occurs at a stage of reversible damage due to oxygen deprivation, whereas the release of lysosomal enzymes may point at irreparable damage."},
